---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ReportBlock from "../components/ReportBlock.astro";

// a single illustrative example, not a live analysis: there is no trained
// combiner yet (SPEC.md section 6 waits on ground truth), so this hero
// report is fixed sample data rather than a claim about any real listing.
const sampleReport = {
  bandLabel: "mixed",
  claimedRating: 4.6,
  adjustedRating: 3.9,
  totalReviewCount: 8431,
  excludedReviewCount: 1208,
  serial: "7F2A-0091",
  rosette: { harmonicA: 5, harmonicB: 8, amplitude: 0.45, strokeColor: "#B08128" },
};
---

<Layout
  title="Verdict"
  description="Verdict reads the reviews your browser already downloaded and shows you which ones do not hold up."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <h1>Not every review was written by a person.</h1>
      <p>
        Verdict reads the reviews your browser has already downloaded and shows you which ones
        do not hold up. It runs on your machine. Nothing about what you shop for is sent
        anywhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-primary" href="/install">Add to Chrome</a>
        <span class="also-on">Also on Firefox, Edge, Brave and Opera.</span>
      </p>
    </div>
    <ReportBlock {...sampleReport} />
  </section>

  <section class="explain">
    <div>
      <h2>It recomputes the rating.</h2>
      <p>
        Suspect reviews come out and the average is recalculated. On a listing showing 4.6
        stars, that number is often somewhere quite different.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>It shows the reasoning.</h2>
      <p>
        Every report opens into the evidence. How the ratings are distributed, when they
        arrived, whether the text repeats, whether they describe the product actually being
        sold. You can disagree with it, which is the point.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>It notices when a listing changed identity.</h2>
      <p>
        Sellers reuse old listings. Reviews praising a phone case end up attached to a hair
        dryer. Verdict reads what the reviews are actually about and tells you how many of them
        belong to something else.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="why">
    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      Fakespot did this for nearly ten years. Mozilla bought it in 2023 and shut it down on
      1 July 2025, and disabled the Review Checker built into Firefox three weeks earlier.
      Their explanation was that it did not fit a model they could sustain. ReviewMeta closed
      before that. Millions of people lost the tool and nothing took its place.
    </p>
    <p>
      Verdict is built differently. Fakespot ran the analysis on its own servers, which meant
      paying to fetch pages at scale and, unavoidably, seeing what everyone was shopping for.
      Verdict does the work in your browser, on pages you have already opened. That removes the
      cost and it removes the surveillance at the same time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="close">
    <p>
      <a class="button-primary" href="/install">Add to Chrome</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .hero {
    display: flex;
    gap: 40px;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    padding: 48px 0;
  }

  .hero-copy {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 280px;
  }

  .hero-copy h1 {
    font-size: 3.25rem;
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    font-family: "Bricolage Grotesque", system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .also-on {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-left: 12px;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .explain {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 24px;
    padding: 32px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .why {
    padding: 32px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .close {
    padding: 32px 0 64px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
</style>
